<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lee Kuan Yew</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1923">1923</a>–<a href="/years/2015">2015</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lee Kuan Yew was the first prime minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a>, holding office from 1959 to 1990. As leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-action-party">People's Action Party</a> he took Singapore into Malaysia in 1963 and, after its separation in 1965, led it as an independent state. His government built Singapore into a prosperous trading and financial centre under the dominance of a single party.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lee-kuan-yew.jpg" alt="Lee Kuan Yew" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: White House Photographic Office. 1974-1977 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Prime_Minister_Lee_Kuan_Yew_of_Singapore_Making_a_Toast_at_a_State_Dinner_Held_in_His_Honor,_1975.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Lee Kuan Yew was born Harry Lee Kuan Yew on 16 September 1923 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a>, then part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/straits-settlements">Straits Settlements</a>, the first child of Lee Chin Koon and Chua Jim Neo, both English-educated third-generation Peranakan Chinese. During the Japanese occupation he was among the Chinese men rounded up in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sook-ching">Sook Ching</a> operation and left the screening site on the second day, later learning that the group of men was likely taken to the beach and executed. In 1946 he sailed for the United Kingdom, studied law at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>, and returned to Singapore in 1950. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> he had met Goh Keng Swee and Toh Chin Chye, and after his return they formed a political party.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Lee co-founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-action-party">People's Action Party</a> (PAP) in 1954, and after it won the 1959 election he became prime minister. He and his colleagues held that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a> could survive only through merger with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malaya">Malaya</a>. On 16 September 1963 he proclaimed Singapore part of Malaysia, together with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sabah">Sabah</a> and Sarawak, and tensions with the federal government over industrialisation and taxes followed; Singapore became independent in 1965.</p><p>As prime minister of the new state he appointed Goh Keng Swee to build the armed forces, introduced conscription in 1967, addressed unemployment through manufacturing, and gave the Corrupt Practices Investigation Bureau greater powers. Critics have described his government as an illiberal democracy that restricted press freedom and public assembly, and from 1968 to 1981 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a> was a de facto <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/one-party-state">one-party state</a>.</p><p>He stepped down on 28 November 1990 after seven election victories, handing the premiership to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goh-chok-tong">Goh Chok Tong</a>, and remained in the Cabinet as Senior Minister until 2004 and Minister Mentor until 2011.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Lee died of pneumonia on 23 March 2015, aged 91. A week of national mourning was announced, during which approximately 1.7 million people paid their respects. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a> he is widely regarded as instrumental in the development of the economy, education system, public housing and healthcare, and the Lee Kuan Yew School of Public Policy at the National University of Singapore bears his name. He argued that communitarianism and limited human rights were necessary for rapid economic development, and he said that he had had to do some nasty things, locking fellows up without trial.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lee Kuan Yew” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Singapore's separation on 9 August 1965, after disputes between the People's Action Party of <b>Lee Kuan Yew</b> and UMNO over Malay privileges and a &quot;Malaysian Malaysia&quot;, reduced the federation to its present form.</blockquote><a href="/empire/malaysia">Malaysia · Into the Present</a></li><li><blockquote>Singapore became a separate crown colony on 1 April 1946, lived through communist insurgency and the riots of 1954–1956, and won full internal self-government on 3 June 1959, days after the People's Action Party under <b>Lee Kuan Yew</b> took the first election held with universal suffrage.</blockquote><a href="/empire/singapore">Singapore · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Full internal self-government came in 1959 as the State of Singapore, with <b>Lee Kuan Yew</b> of the People's Action Party as prime minister, while Britain kept defence and foreign affairs.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-singapore">Colony of Singapore</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/malaysia">Malaysia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/malaysia">Malaysia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=1.3,103.8&amp;year=1982" aria-label="Singapore on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7433 2115 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Lee Kuan Yew"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7883 2385h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7883 2385h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=1.3,103.8&amp;year=1982">What was here in 1982? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
